% Physical equality with a value made in the comparison itself
% (docs/plans/plan_bug_analyses.md, A6): mini-analyze -bugs, over the facts
% of Closure_facts (FACTS.md):
%   phys_compare(I, F, Op)  the call I of == or != (Op) is in F
%   fresh_operand(I, What)  ... and one of its sides is made there: a
%                           constructor applied (What its name), a tuple,
%                           a record, an array, a string, a float
%   compared(I, V)          V is one of I's two sides
% and says:
%   always(I, F, Op, What)  the answer of I is known before it runs:
%                           false for ==, true for !=
%   stale(I, F, H)          I compares by identity a value kept (read from
%                           a field that is given it, a cache's last one)
%                           with H, which is changed in place: the same
%                           block before and after the change, I does not
%                           see it
%
%     if !drawn != Some !model then paint ()      (eb5378a5^, Window.ml)
%
% Some !model is a block made at that line, so no other value is it, and
% the test is always true: the screen was painted again each tick, the
% program never waited. Meant was what = says, or the model compared:
% (match !drawn with Some m -> m != !model | None -> true). Which then
% never saw mini-emacs's model change: its top window, one record whose
% fields a key changes, so the screen was not painted again after the
% first (282b689b; the fix, a box made again after each key).
%
% design:
% The answer is read off the program's text, no fixpoint: what is made in
% place is the expression's, which the front end sees and the facts keep.
% The rule is here, not in Closure_facts, so that -bugs is one program of
% rules and its next checks join the same relations: the identity of a
% value changed in place (a cache comparing by == what is mutated, the
% other half of eb5378a5) needs the pointer analysis, store(V, Fld, _)
% and pts(V, S) for the S compared. A constant constructor and [] are
% numbers, not blocks, and a closure may be static: none is fresh.
% stale needs points-to: a side read from a field (!model) that the
% comparing function's own store_in gives the object the other side may
% be (the cache's refresh, drawn := Some !model: a box's field, through
% the Some), and a store_in into that object (a field set, not a block's
% making). Identity on a value
% changed in place is often right (a graph's nodes): what is wrong is a
% cache of the last one seen, its comparison meant to say "changed", in
% a function that does not know the value's type ('model): the first
% sweep's 16 lines were identities meant, rio's windows, ed's marks.
% A picture changed by its bytes (fbfc0317's) is not seen: Bytes'
% writes are no store_in.
%
% others:
% OCaml's compiler warns of none of this; Java's == on a new Integer, or
% on strings, is the same bug, which FindBugs (David Hovemeyer, William
% Pugh, 2004) reported by a pattern of the bytecode, as here by one of the
% tree: a "bug pattern", their name for a check that small.
%
% References: David Hovemeyer, William Pugh, "Finding bugs is easy"
% (OOPSLA 2004 companion).

always(I, F, Op, W) :- phys_compare(I, F, Op), fresh_operand(I, W).

% (identity of a value whose type the function does not know, 'model:
% whether it is changed in place is the caller's, and identity cannot say
% "changed"; a function that knows the type, a window, a frame, a line,
% means identity, "another one")
generic_compare(I, F, V) :- phys_compare(I, F, _), compared(I, V), \+ vtype(V, _).
% (V read from C, an object the comparing function itself writes into a
% place: the last value kept, put there again, a cache's refresh, Some m
% in drawn)
refreshed(C, F) :- generic_compare(_, F, _), store_in(_, _, W, F), pts(W, C).
keeps(I, V, H) :-
  generic_compare(I, F, V), assign_load_field(V, K, _), pts(K, C),
  refreshed(C, F), pts(V, H).
changed_in_place(H) :- store_in(O, _, _, _), pts(O, H).
stale(I, F, H) :-
  keeps(I, V, H), phys_compare(I, F, _), compared(I, V2), V2 \= V,
  pts(V2, H), changed_in_place(H), reach(F).
